    <h2>The first visit</h2>
    <p>Bring your new arrival, any paperwork from the breeder or shelter, and your questions (write them down: everyone forgets one). We give a full check, start or continue the vaccine course, check for fleas, ear mites and worms, and talk about food, toilet training, teething and sleep.</p>
    <h2>The first year, step by step</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>8 weeks:</strong> first exam and first vaccines, worming, microchip.</li>
        <li><strong>11 to 12 weeks:</strong> second vaccines, a weight check, socialising tips.</li>
        <li><strong>15 to 16 weeks:</strong> final puppy or kitten vaccines and rabies.</li>
        <li><strong>5 to 12 months:</strong> spay or neuter, and a teeth and growth check.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Raising a pet who likes the vet</h2>
    <p>Juniper, one of our nurses, runs short “happy visits” for puppies and kittens: come in, step on the scales, get a fuss and a treat, and leave without anything unpleasant happening at all. It is the single best thing you can do for every visit that comes after.</p>
    <p class="callout">Puppies and kittens can go downhill quickly. If yours is not eating, is very quiet, or has diarrhoea for more than a day, call us that day.</p>
